(a) Definitions. [The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this section, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:]
New capital purchases means that which would meet the following criteria:
(1) Purchase would exceed $300.00;
(2) Purchase would not be used to make a repair or maintain a normal operation of the city.
Stock purchases means items that city employees use such as the following:
(1) Office supplies;
(2) Chemicals;
(3) Janitorial supplies;
(4) Lab equipment and supplies (water and sewer plants);
(5) Water pipe and fittings;
(6) Sewer pipe and fittings;
(7) Fuel;
(8) Uniforms;
(9) Garden supplies (grass seed, straw, topsoil and fertilizer);
(10) Gravel and asphalt.
These items should be on hand at a reasonable inventory level to insure availability to keep production going as smoothly as possible.
Maintenance and repair purchases means purchases will maintain the city owned property, equipment, vehicles, parks, buildings, streets, infrastructure and treatment plants.
(b) Procedures.
(1) Purchasing agent. The city manager shall be the purchasing agent for the city. The city manager may at his discretion authorize another city employee to make purchases for the city. The purchasing agent shall arrange and negotiate the purchase of or contract for all equipment, supply contractual services for the city, and sell or otherwise dispose of all surplus city equipment and supplies. The purchasing agent shall determine that items are necessary and the quantity requested is appropriate.
(2) Lease purchases. The city council shall approve all lease-purchase contracts which would extend beyond the current fiscal year.
(3) Purchase orders. All purchases will require issuance of a prenumbered purchase order from city hall.
(4) Bids/price quotations. The following dollar amounts shall be applied in determining the process to be used in obtaining bids/price quotations:
a. Up to $5,000.00—Verbal quotation.
b. $5,001.00 to $10,000.00—Written quotation.
c. Over $10,000.00—Sealed bids.
(5) Verbal quotations. A written record will be retained for a period of one year after the purchase has been made.
(6) Written bids. The city manager will oversee all written bids and follow the following requirements:
a. Write the specifications and advertise for bids;
b. Mail the specifications and bid request to prospective bidders;
c. Maintain complete records of any bid deposits received;
d. Advertise the bid opening in the legal organ prior to opening;
e. Hold a public bid opening at the time and place announced;
f. In the event the low bidder is not awarded the contract, the reason should be documented.
(7) Agreements. The appropriate municipal official and the successful bidder must sign a written agreement after the contract award. A copy of the signed contract should be maintained in the city's files. Any bid deposits should be returned to unsuccessful bidders after the contract is signed and materials returned to city by the unsuccessful bidder.
(8) State contract use. The purchasing agent may use State of Georgia contracts for purchasing any products that are available to local governments. By using this method, the necessity of bidding items over $10,000.00 will be eliminated due to the fact that the state has already bid the item.
(9) Brand name purchase. The purchasing agent may elect purchase of a brand name product or service when the goods comprise a major brand system, program, or service previously selected by the city and due to operational effectiveness, future enhancements or additions, or maintenance and storage of spare parts preclude the mixing of brands, manufactures, etc.
(10) Sole vendor products. The department head and/or city manager will make an effort to determine if the price is reasonable. In the event the department head and/or city manager feels a sole vendor is taking advantage of the city it will be timely placed on the agenda for the mayor and city council to consider.
(11) Emergency purchasing. The city will make an effort to try and keep a reasonable amount of stock on hand at all times. However, in the event an emergency arises the city will obtain the necessary items from vendors that the city does business with on a normal basis without going through the bidding process.
(12) Collaborative purchasing. The purchasing agent may elect to purchase products or services through the collaborative purchasing process which will not require bids.
(13) Procedures for new capital purchases.
a. City manager purchasing limit. The city manager shall have a $5,000.00 purchasing limit.
b. Department heads purchasing limits. Department heads shall have a $500.00 purchasing limit with an appropriate purchase order issued at city hall. Department heads should get prices from at least two vendors prior to purchasing the item.
c. Emergency approval by mayor. The mayor shall have the authority to approve a department head's purchase not to exceed $5,000.00 in the event of an emergency if the city manager, or his or her designee, is unavailable to review the expenditure request.
d. Council approval. The city council shall approve all new capital purchases over the limits set for department heads and the city manager.
(14) Procedures for stock, maintenance and repair purchases; approval. The mayor and council will approve the purchase of stock, maintenance and repair purchase in the annual budget. The city manager will monitor these purchases to assure that appropriate funds are available prior to issuing purchase orders.
(Policy of 12-13-99; Ord. No. 2012-0611-3, § 1, 6-11-12)
